Nit: I don't think the kernels actually rely on the padding being zero. Output row i of a tile multiply comes only from A row i, and column n only from B column n. `qk_vnni_128x4` copies rows 0..3 of `s16` out, and the row-major transpose only emits columns 0..3 as rows of `s`, so whatever is in the padding never reaches a kept score. The zero-fill still makes sense (the tile loads read defined memory, and the dropped rows stay deterministic). But "rely" may lead someone to add a per-pack memset later. Maybe:

```suggestion
        // resize() zero-fills new slots, so the padding the packers do not
        // write is defined. No kept score reads it: the kernels drop the
        // padding rows (VNNI) and columns (row-major) of their result tiles.
```

The same softening could apply to "zero padding the caller keeps zero" at `amx_attn_iface.hpp:241-242`.

_How I checked: TDPBF16PS semantics as the header's own Note states (iface:104-105, "output row i of a tile multiply comes from row i of the A operand"), plus the copy-out loop at `amx_attn.cpp:287-289` (rows 0..3) and the transpose, whose store loop `k < 4` (`:215-219`) writes only rows 0..3 of `s` (it loads whole tile rows, but only those four output rows are stored)._

